From da29edc7c371d084d65dfa17151d1d06bf61d8d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FreeholdOS Build <build@freeholdos.local>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 16:31:19 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] grab: an impact after a short free fall is a set-down and
 does not lock, with a strictness setting

The impact route locked on every acceleration peak of 30 m/s^2 or more, so a set-down of about 3 cm locked the phone (GRB-018). A phone that is set down falls freely for a moment before it lands and a grab has no free fall: 12 staged grabs had none, 29 set-downs from 3 cm to a hard push had one of 16 to 163 ms (GRB-019, replayed through this code at 50 Hz in five phases).

GrabDetector tracks the runs of samples with a magnitude below 4 m/s^2 and, at an impact, takes the longest one that ended within 400 ms before it. An impact after no free fall, or after one of at least freeFallLongMs, locks; after a shorter one it does not, and the rest of its pulse is not judged again for 250 ms (the pull rule still sees it). A limit of zero keeps the old behaviour.

The new setting grab_impact_level has three values: 0 every impact locks, 1 (default) a set-down does not lock (limit 250 ms, a fall of about 30 cm), 2 as 1 with twice the impact peak, for running. The impact log line now names the free fall, the verdict and the level.
